1
00:00:00,140 --> 00:00:04,460
April 20th, 2010, the Deepwater Horizon drill floor,

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:06,520
roughly an hour before the well blows.

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:11,400
Senior tool pusher Jason Anderson wasn't even supposed to be on board tonight.

4
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:13,160
His replacement never showed up,

5
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:17,440
so he stayed, cleaned out his locker and spent one more shift with his crew.

6
00:00:18,980 --> 00:00:23,080
He calls a colleague off shift in his cabin and tells him the well just passed its final

7
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:25,220
pressure test. A good test,

8
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:27,180
he says. No signs of trouble.

9
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,500
It was not a good test. Less than an hour later,

10
00:00:32,920 --> 00:00:35,040
mud and gas erupt onto the rig floor.

11
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,820
Two explosions ripped through the Deepwater Horizon.

12
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:40,120
Eleven men on that drill floor,

13
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:41,520
including Jason Anderson,

14
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,060
will not survive the night.

15
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:48,700
Two years later, the company that owned that well stood in a federal courtroom and pleaded

16
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,540
guilty, guilty to 11 counts of manslaughter,

17
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:53,620
one for every man who died.

18
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:57,620
This is the real story of how a nightmare well,

19
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:02,440
an ignored warning, and a six -word email ended in the largest oil spill in American

20
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:06,520
history. You're watching Black Box Breakdown.

21
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:09,440
BP pled guilty to killing 11 men.

22
00:01:10,900 --> 00:01:13,220
Chapter 1. The rig, the well,

23
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:17,920
and the three companies. The Deepwater Horizon was a floating,

24
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,860
drilling rig built in South Korea in 2001,

25
00:01:21,140 --> 00:01:23,060
owned by a company called Transocean,

26
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:25,940
and leased by an oil company you've definitely heard of,

27
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:31,520
BP. In April of 2010, it was drilling BP's Macondo Well

28
00:01:31,520 --> 00:01:36,440
about 41 miles off the coast of Louisiana in 5 ,000 feet of water.

29
00:01:37,940 --> 00:01:40,060
126 people were on board that night.

30
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:42,220
79 of them worked for Transocean.

31
00:01:42,660 --> 00:01:45,340
The rest worked for BP, and for a third company,

32
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:49,340
Halliburton, hired to pump the cement that was supposed to seal the well shut.

33
00:01:50,360 --> 00:01:52,520
Three companies won well,

34
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,300
and when it failed, all three would end up blaming each other in court.

35
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:00,160
BP owned the well and made the final calls.

36
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,020
Transocean owned the rig and its safety equipment,

37
00:02:03,300 --> 00:02:07,220
including a massive valve at the bottom of the sea called a blowout preventer.

38
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:10,260
Halliburton designed and pumped the cement job.

39
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:15,100
Renting that rig cost BP about $1 million a day.

40
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:21,240
And by April 20th, the well was five to six weeks behind schedule and tens of

41
00:02:21,240 --> 00:02:22,560
millions of dollars over budget.

42
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:28,420
One BP engineer put it plainly in an email six days before the blowout.

43
00:02:29,560 --> 00:02:30,740
This has been a nightmare well,

44
00:02:31,140 --> 00:02:32,900
which is everyone all over the place.

45
00:02:34,380 --> 00:02:37,360
Chapter two, six warnings and one who cares.

46
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:40,120
To finish the well faster,

47
00:02:40,500 --> 00:02:42,920
BP chose the cheapest of two casing designs,

48
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:45,020
faster to install, cheaper,

49
00:02:45,660 --> 00:02:47,820
and harder to seal with cement at this depth.

50
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:54,200
A Halliburton engineer ran the numbers and warned BP the cement job needed at least 21

51
00:02:54,200 --> 00:02:56,520
centering devices, called centralizers,

52
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:00,320
to seal properly. BP used six.

53
00:03:01,880 --> 00:03:05,000
An engineer named Brian Morrell raised the concern to a colleague,

54
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:10,160
Brett Kokalas. Kokalas wrote back four words that would later be read aloud in a federal

55
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:12,800
courtroom. But who cares?

56
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:16,600
It's done. End of story will probably be fine.

57
00:03:18,100 --> 00:03:19,300
Four days before the blowout,

58
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,140
a specialized crew arrived to run one more test.

59
00:03:22,740 --> 00:03:27,920
A cement bond log. The one test that could have proven for certain whether the seal actually

60
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:32,940
held. On April 20th, BP canceled that test and

61
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:38,020
sent the crew home. Investigators later found the cement itself never should have

62
00:03:38,020 --> 00:03:41,680
passed. An early version of the mixture was tested and approved.

63
00:03:42,280 --> 00:03:44,440
But it wasn't the version actually pumped into the well.

64
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:50,680
Engineers hired years later to recreate that exact cement job in a lab could not get

65
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,800
it to hold, either. Chapter three,

66
00:03:54,260 --> 00:03:57,940
the night of April 20th. At 11 in the morning,

67
00:03:58,260 --> 00:04:00,840
the crew runs a test meant to prove the well is sealed.

68
00:04:01,420 --> 00:04:02,720
The result doesn't make sense.

69
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:05,360
At five in the afternoon,

70
00:04:05,640 --> 00:04:09,860
they try again. One gauge reads 1 ,400 pounds per square inch,

71
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:12,220
where a passing test should read almost nothing.

72
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,000
They run a second test on a different line.

73
00:04:16,420 --> 00:04:17,800
That one reads close to zero.

74
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:20,820
The crew decides the first bad reading was a fluke,

75
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:23,260
a phenomenon they'd nicknamed the bladder effect.

76
00:04:24,840 --> 00:04:29,100
Investigators later found no scientific evidence the bladder effect is even real.

77
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:31,300
It was a fatal misunderstanding,

78
00:04:31,800 --> 00:04:33,440
not a real physical phenomenon.

79
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:36,260
Believing the well was sealed,

80
00:04:36,700 --> 00:04:38,340
the crew begins the final step,

81
00:04:38,700 --> 00:04:41,480
swapping out heavy drilling mud for lighter seawater.

82
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:45,580
That mud was the last thing holding the well's pressure down.

83
00:04:46,240 --> 00:04:49,600
Now, only the cement stood between the crew and the reservoir below,

84
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:51,740
and the cement had already failed.

85
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:56,200
At 9 .41, the driller closes an emergency valve,

86
00:04:56,380 --> 00:04:57,720
trying to shut the well in.

87
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:59,880
It barely slows anything down.

88
00:05:00,860 --> 00:05:03,560
At 9 .46, pressure spikes.

89
00:05:04,180 --> 00:05:06,720
Mud, then gas, erupts onto the rig floor.

90
00:05:07,220 --> 00:05:08,660
The gas overwhelms the system,

91
00:05:08,660 --> 00:05:12,040
built to vent it safely, and pours out across the deck instead.

92
00:05:13,320 --> 00:05:16,720
At 9 .49, the gas cloud finds an ignition source.

93
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:20,060
Seven minutes later, a second,

94
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:24,780
larger explosion tears through the rig and fire engulfs the deep water horizon.

95
00:05:25,880 --> 00:05:28,300
Chapter four, eleven names.

96
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:31,500
With less than five minutes' warning,

97
00:05:31,920 --> 00:05:33,660
the crew evacuates by lifeboat,

98
00:05:34,020 --> 00:05:35,760
by life raft, and for some,

99
00:05:35,960 --> 00:05:39,340
by jumping straight into the Gulf of Mexico from a burning rig.

100
00:05:41,180 --> 00:05:44,780
One hundred fifteen of the one hundred twenty -six people on board made it home.

101
00:05:46,340 --> 00:05:49,400
Eleven did not. Jason Anderson.

102
00:05:50,140 --> 00:05:51,940
Arendelle Burkine. Donald Clark.

103
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:53,960
Stephen Curtis. Gordon Jones.

104
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:57,120
Wyatt Kemp. Carl Kleppinger,

105
00:05:57,260 --> 00:05:59,820
Jr. Keith Manuel. Dewey Revett,

106
00:06:00,100 --> 00:06:02,320
who had worked for Transocean for twenty -nine years.

107
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:07,860
Shane Rosto. Adam Wise. Gordon Jones called his pregnant wife,

108
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:10,780
Michelle, about ten minutes before the explosion,

109
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:13,100
three days before their sixth anniversary.

110
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,540
Their son, Maxwell, was born two months later.

111
00:06:17,940 --> 00:06:21,720
Investigators would later find that every one of these deaths traced back to the same

112
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:24,820
chain of decisions, a well -designed to save time,

113
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:26,700
a warning that went unheeded,

114
00:06:27,040 --> 00:06:30,740
a test that was misread. Eleven counts,

115
00:06:31,280 --> 00:06:34,060
eleven names. Chapter five,

116
00:06:34,500 --> 00:06:38,180
eighty -seven days. Two days after the explosion,

117
00:06:38,620 --> 00:06:42,700
still burning, the deepwater horizon sank to the bottom of the Gulf of Mexico.

118
00:06:43,820 --> 00:06:45,920
It was still attached to the well below,

119
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:47,540
and the well kept flowing.

120
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:52,180
For eighty -seven days, oil poured into the Gulf of Mexico,

121
00:06:52,640 --> 00:06:56,700
completely unchecked. BP tried a containment dome.

122
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:59,440
It failed. A procedure nicknamed the top kill.

123
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:05,560
It failed. A junk shot of shredded rubber and golf balls pumped into the blowout preventer.

124
00:07:05,780 --> 00:07:10,840
That failed too. By the time a new capping stack finally sealed the well

125
00:07:10,840 --> 00:07:15,840
on July 15th, nearly five million barrels of oil had poured into the Gulf of Mexico.

126
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:21,800
It remains the largest accidental marine oil spill in the history of the petroleum industry.

127
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:25,460
Skimmer boats, miles of floating boom,

128
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:28,340
controlled burns on the surface of the ocean itself.

129
00:07:29,900 --> 00:07:33,300
The well was finally, permanently sealed in mid -September,

130
00:07:33,960 --> 00:07:36,140
152 days after it first blew.

131
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:39,300
Chapter six, the guilty plea.

132
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:44,140
In November of 2012, BP agreed to plead guilty.

133
00:07:44,820 --> 00:07:46,300
Fourteen federal criminal counts.

134
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:49,540
Eleven felony counts of manslaughter.

135
00:07:50,180 --> 00:07:51,520
One for every man who died.

136
00:07:51,880 --> 00:07:55,400
Plus obstructing Congress and violating clean water law.

137
00:07:56,960 --> 00:07:59,280
BP paid four billion dollars in criminal penalties.

138
00:07:59,680 --> 00:08:02,960
At the time, the largest criminal resolution in American history.

139
00:08:04,380 --> 00:08:06,960
Two years later, a federal judge went further,

140
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:08,920
ruling that BP acted with,

141
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:12,860
quote, gross negligence and willful misconduct and split the blame.

142
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:17,780
BP, 67%, Transocean, 30%,

143
00:08:17,780 --> 00:08:23,060
Halliburton, 3%. Transocean pleaded guilty to a water pollution

144
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:26,440
charge. Halliburton pleaded guilty to destroying evidence.

145
00:08:27,880 --> 00:08:31,740
In 2016, a federal judge approved a separate civil settlement,

146
00:08:32,440 --> 00:08:37,740
20 .8 billion dollars, the largest settlement with a single company in American history.

147
00:08:38,900 --> 00:08:41,400
Across every fine, every settlement,

148
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:46,400
every clean -up cost, BP's total bill for that one well has now passed 61

149
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:51,460
billion dollars. Two BP supervisors on the rig that night were charged with

150
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:53,740
manslaughter on top of the corporate plea.

151
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:57,660
Neither one was ever convicted of it.

152
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:01,520
Chapter seven, who paid and who didn't.

153
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:04,240
One of those two supervisors,

154
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:07,500
Donald Vadrine, pleaded guilty to a single misdemeanor.

155
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:12,740
Ten months probation. He died in 2017 after a battle with cancer.

156
00:09:14,220 --> 00:09:15,520
The other, Robert Kaluza,

157
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:18,760
took his case to trial and was acquitted outright by a jury.

158
00:09:20,420 --> 00:09:25,020
No individual on that rig or in that company was ever convicted of manslaughter.

159
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:27,820
Only the corporation pleaded guilty to that.

160
00:09:29,200 --> 00:09:30,960
BP's chief executive at the time,

161
00:09:31,340 --> 00:09:34,700
Tony Heywood, gave one interview that followed him for the rest of his career.

162
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:38,260
There's no one who wants this over more than I do.

163
00:09:38,260 --> 00:09:42,660
I'd like my life back. Eleven men had just died.

164
00:09:43,400 --> 00:09:44,420
Heywood later apologized,

165
00:09:44,820 --> 00:09:46,880
calling his own comment hurtful and thoughtless.

166
00:09:47,340 --> 00:09:48,820
He resigned five months later.

167
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:53,260
He went on to chair two more energy companies over the next decade.

168
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:56,300
Chapter eight, still paying.

169
00:09:57,860 --> 00:10:00,940
Sixteen years later, BP is still cutting checks.

170
00:10:02,480 --> 00:10:06,720
Louisiana alone is set to receive over seven billion dollars in restoration funding.

171
00:10:06,720 --> 00:10:09,720
The last scheduled payments don't land until the 2030s.

172
00:10:11,640 --> 00:10:16,720
In June of 2026, the Gulf Coast Restoration Council approved another 400

173
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:19,900
million dollars for 19 new restoration projects.

174
00:10:21,300 --> 00:10:26,520
A 2024 study found deep sea coral colonies in the Gulf still visibly suffering more

175
00:10:26,520 --> 00:10:27,900
than a decade after the spill.

176
00:10:28,440 --> 00:10:32,160
Some researchers estimate full recovery could take 50 to 100 years.

177
00:10:33,460 --> 00:10:37,900
The Gulf's dolphin population suffered the largest die -off ever recorded in the region.

178
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:43,780
Well, that was supposed to save six weeks of schedule is still costing someone something

179
00:10:43,780 --> 00:10:48,920
every single year. Every official investigation into this disaster

180
00:10:48,920 --> 00:10:50,780
reached the same root finding.

181
00:10:51,300 --> 00:10:55,420
A cement barrier meant to hold back millions of years of pressure failed.

182
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:59,220
But every report also agreed on something else.

183
00:10:59,700 --> 00:11:01,860
That failure was never just a technical problem.

184
00:11:02,260 --> 00:11:07,260
It was the last link in a chain of decisions each one made to save a little time or

185
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:11,320
a little money. 11 men paid for that chain with their lives.

186
00:11:11,860 --> 00:11:14,300
And the Gulf of Mexico is still paying for it today.

187
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:18,760
BP had a six -word email warning them this well was a nightmare.

188
00:11:19,180 --> 00:11:20,700
And who cares? It's done.

189
